CAT = "cemapEthics"; SEG = "ETH"
Q = [
("An ethical theory that judges an action by its results for the greatest number of people is:",
 "Utilitarianism", ["Deontology", "Virtue ethics", "Ethical relativism"],
 "Utilitarianism is a consequentialist theory: the right act produces the best overall outcome. Deontology judges the act itself against duties."),
("An adviser refuses to mislead a client even when it would help the firm, because lying is wrong in itself. This reflects which approach to ethics?",
 "Deontology", ["Utilitarianism", "Ethical egoism", "Consequentialism"],
 "Deontology, associated with Kant, holds that some duties, such as honesty, apply whatever the consequences."),
("Virtue ethics asks mainly:",
 "What a good person would do", ["Which rule the FCA has written", "Which option earns the most", "What most people would vote for"],
 "Virtue ethics, from Aristotle, focuses on character traits such as honesty and fairness rather than on rules or outcomes."),
("A compliance-only mindset asks 'Is it allowed?'. An ethical approach also asks:",
 "Is it the right thing to do?", ["Will it raise this month's sales?", "Will the regulator ever notice?", "Is it what competitors do?"],
 "Compliance checks the rules; ethics also asks whether an action is fair and right for the client. The Consumer Duty expects both."),
("Personal values are best described as:",
 "Beliefs that guide choices", ["Laws passed by Parliament", "Rules set by the employer", "Targets for annual bonuses"],
 "Values are a person's lasting beliefs about what matters. Morals apply them to right and wrong, and ethics turns them into standards of conduct."),
("Which UK law protects workers who make a protected disclosure about wrongdoing?",
 "Public Interest Disclosure Act 1998", ["Data Protection Act 2018", "Consumer Rights Act 2015", "Financial Services Act 2012"],
 "PIDA 1998 protects whistleblowers from unfair dismissal or detriment when they report wrongdoing in the public interest."),
("An adviser offers a lender's underwriter cash to approve a weak application. This is most likely an offence under:",
 "The Bribery Act 2010", ["The Consumer Rights Act 2015", "The Proceeds of Crime Act 2002", "The Data Protection Act 2018"],
 "Offering a payment to induce someone to act improperly is bribery under the Bribery Act 2010. Firms can also commit an offence by failing to prevent bribery by people acting for them."),
("Continuing professional development (CPD) is mainly intended to:",
 "Keep skills up to date", ["Replace the original qualification", "Increase sales targets", "Count hours only"],
 "CPD maintains competence as rules, products and markets change. Good CPD is planned, reflective and relevant to the role."),
("A firm with a strong ethical culture is most likely to:",
 "Encourage staff to raise concerns", ["Punish anyone who questions sales", "Reward sales above everything", "Hide complaints from the board"],
 "Open challenge, fair incentives and leadership by example are signs of good culture. A sales-at-all-costs culture leads to mis-selling."),
("Case: Jamal notices a colleague changing clients' income figures to get mortgages approved. What should he do first?",
 "Report it internally", ["Ignore it to avoid trouble", "Confront the clients", "Change the figures himself"],
 "Falsifying income is mortgage fraud and harms clients. Jamal should raise it internally, and can go to the FCA if the firm does not act."),
("Case: Jamal fears he may lose his job if he reports his colleague's mortgage fraud. What protects him?",
 "Whistleblowing law and FCA rules", ["Keeping the evidence at home", "Telling the affected clients first", "Waiting for an internal audit"],
 "PIDA protects workers making protected disclosures, and FCA rules require firms not to treat whistleblowers unfairly."),
("Case: Jamal's manager says the fraud is fine because 'everyone does it'. Which ethical point applies?",
 "Common practice does not make it right", ["Majority behaviour sets the ethical standard", "It is legal if widespread", "Only the FCA decides what is right"],
 "An action's wrongness does not depend on how many people do it. Ethical and legal standards still apply."),
("Which behaviour shows professional integrity?",
 "Admitting a mistake to the client", ["Quietly hiding an error to save face", "Blaming the lender for it", "Waiting for the client to notice"],
 "Integrity means honesty even when it is costly. Owning up to mistakes and putting them right builds trust."),
("Placing the client's interests ahead of the adviser's own commission is an example of:",
 "Professional ethics", ["Poor business judgement", "A breach of contract", "Market abuse"],
 "Professional codes of ethics in financial services ask members to put clients' interests first and act with integrity."),
("Which is an ethical dilemma rather than a simple compliance question?",
 "A permitted sale that may not suit", ["Whether a particular rule exists", "The deadline for a regulatory report", "The date of an FCA rule change"],
 "A dilemma arises when choices conflict, such as a permitted sale that may not be in the client's best interests. Codes of ethics help resolve these."),
]
